From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: drop the irq mapping on unbind

Re-probing this driver always fails:

    irq: type mismatch, failed to map hwirq-203 for interrupt-controller@17000000!
    qcom-soundwire 6ab0000.soundwire: error -EINVAL: request_irq(0) 0x0 qcom_swrm_irq_handler
    qcom-soundwire 6ab0000.soundwire: Failed to request soundwire irq
    qcom-soundwire 6ab0000.soundwire: probe with driver qcom-soundwire failed with error -22

of_irq_get() creates the virq mapping from the device tree, which says
IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH, and the driver then requests the handler with
IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING, overriding it. That is harmless exactly once.

The mapping outlives an unbind. On the next probe of_irq_get() asks the domain
for a level-high mapping, irq_create_fwspec_mapping() finds the surviving
edge-rising one, and refuses it -- returning virq 0 rather than an error. The
driver only rejects negative values, so 0 goes straight into
devm_request_threaded_irq() and comes back as a confusing -EINVAL.

Dispose of the mapping when the driver lets go, so the next probe builds a
fresh one from the device tree and is free to override the type again. The
action is registered before the handler is requested, so devres unwinds them
in the right order: free_irq() first, then the dispose.

Also reject virq 0 where it is obtained, so a future mapping failure is
reported as itself instead of as a request_irq() error.

Nothing unbinds this driver on a Qualcomm SoC today, which is why this has
never been hit. It becomes reachable as soon as the audio DSP restarting
takes the SoundWire masters down with the clock provider they depend on.

---
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -1530,6 +1530,31 @@
 DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE(swrm_reg);
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * of_irq_get() creates the virq mapping, and this driver then overrides the
+ * trigger type the device tree asked for -- the nodes say IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH
+ * while the handler below is requested IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING. That is harmless
+ * exactly once. The mapping outlives an unbind, so on the next probe
+ * of_irq_get() asks the domain for a level-high mapping, finds the surviving
+ * edge-rising one, and irq_create_fwspec_mapping() rejects it:
+ *
+ *   irq: type mismatch, failed to map hwirq-203 for interrupt-controller@...
+ *
+ * It reports that as virq 0, not as an error, so the driver would go on to
+ * request_irq(0) and fail with a misleading -EINVAL.
+ *
+ * Drop the mapping when the driver lets go, so the next probe builds a fresh
+ * one from the device tree and is free to override the type again. Registered
+ * before the handler is requested, so devres releases them in the right order:
+ * free_irq() first, then this.
+ */
+static void qcom_swrm_dispose_irq(void *data)
+{
+	struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl = data;
+
+	irq_dispose_mapping(ctrl->irq);
+}
+
 static int qcom_swrm_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -1577,11 +1602,17 @@
 	}
 
 	ctrl->irq = of_irq_get(dev->of_node, 0);
-	if (ctrl->irq < 0) {
-		ret = ctrl->irq;
+	if (ctrl->irq <= 0) {
+		/* 0 means the mapping failed; it is not a usable interrupt. */
+		ret = ctrl->irq ? ctrl->irq : -EINVAL;
+		dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to map the soundwire irq\n");
 		goto err_init;
 	}
 
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, qcom_swrm_dispose_irq, ctrl);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_init;
+
 	ctrl->hclk = devm_clk_get(dev, "iface");
 	if (IS_ERR(ctrl->hclk)) {
 		ret = dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(ctrl->hclk), "unable to get iface clock\n");
